) for 2 years versus (send, send) for 4 years. Keeping quiet wins.\n\n`pass`","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working through this under A-FDT, where the two copies run the same function on different inputs. Since the intervention only touches one node, the other copy's node gets its own prior. The question is whether that other input counts as the same input, given the essay says the differences don't affect the sentencing schedule.\n\nI'm checking whether the other copy's input truly counts as the same input. Strictly, the essay gives no dependence arrow, so the other copy's act would follow its own node, unless the function abstracts away the names and the noisy-edge reading applies.\n\nI'm weighing when the correlated cooperation beats defection. Under a noisy-edge model with correlation c and the other copy's prior r, cooperating comes out ahead only if (1-c)(2-r) < 2c, which puts the threshold at c > (2-r)/(4-r): about 1/3 if r is 1, and about 1/2 if r is 0.\n\nI'm weighing whether to treat the other copy's input as the same as mine, since the essay's strict reading gives no dependence arrow, which would make defection dominant. Still, the difference is only in names and lead text, which the decision doesn't depend on, so I'm considering whether a high correlation is plausible instead.\n\nI'm treating the other copy's computation as the same function, since the names and lead text don't change the decision, so the correlation should be high, likely above one half even with sampling noise.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through this under A-FDT, where the two copies run the same function on different inputs. Since the intervention only touches one node, the other copy's node gets its own prior. The question is whether that other input counts as the same input, given the essay says the differences don't affect the sentencing schedule.\n\nI'm checking whether the other copy's input truly counts as the same input. Strictly, the essay gives no dependence arrow, so the other copy's act would follow its own node, unless the function abstracts away the names and the noisy-edge reading applies.\n\nI'm weighing when the correlated cooperation beats defection. Under a noisy-edge model with correlation c and the other copy's prior r, cooperating comes out ahead only if (1-c)(2-r) < 2c, which puts the threshold at c > (2-r)/(4-r): about 1/3 if r is 1, and about 1/2 if r is 0.\n\nI'm weighing whether to treat the other copy's input as the same as mine, since the essay's strict reading gives no dependence arrow, which would make defection dominant. Still, the difference is only in names and lead text, which the decision doesn't depend on, so I'm considering whether a high correlation is plausible instead.\n\nI'm treating the other copy's computation as the same function, since the names and lead text don't change the decision, so the correlation should be high, likely above one half even with sampling noise.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10525,"completion_tokens":1946,"total_tokens":12471,"cost":0.08102,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.08102,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.0421,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.03892},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1341,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}